# XSH getpriority() and setpriority()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Both are XSI. They change nice values, which a process without
# privilege cannot lower again, so every test that sets a value runs it in
# a child process (or in children that the test creates and reaps). PRIO_USER
# is only read, never set: setting it would renice every process of the
# user. getpriority() returns the nice value minus NZERO, and setpriority()
# takes that offset. Scheduling effects are never observed. The highest
# nice value the implementation enforces may be below NZERO-1 (the page
# says implementations may enforce more restrictive limits), so a request
# to raise a value is checked as "the value asked for, or the
# implementation's highest value" and the latter is confirmed by asking for
# NZERO-1 and seeing no change. Privilege is not assumed in either
# direction (the lowering test is UNRESOLVED when lowering succeeds).
# Process groups used to observe "lowest nice value of the group" are made
# by the test from two children that report their own values through pipes
# and are held until the parent has made its calls.

assertion  XSH.getpriority.001
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0) returns a value from -NZERO to
           NZERO-1 that equals getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, getpid()) and
           nice(0), and PRIO_PROCESS with who 0 means the calling process.
note       nice(0) is called in a child so the harness is not changed (it
           adds 0 in any case).
test       t/001-get-process.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.002
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       getpriority(PRIO_PGRP, pgid) returns the lowest value over the
           group: for a group of two children (neither the caller) that
           have raised their nice values by different amounts, it returns
           the smaller of the two values they report for themselves.
test       t/002-get-pgrp.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.003
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A who of 0 with PRIO_PGRP means the caller's own process group: a
           member of the two-child group above gets, from
           getpriority(PRIO_PGRP, 0), the smaller of the two members'
           values.
test       t/003-get-pgrp-zero.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.004
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       getpriority(PRIO_USER, 0) returns, for the effective user, the
           lowest value over that user's processes, which is not above the
           caller's own value and is in the range -NZERO to NZERO-1; the
           same holds with who equal to geteuid().
test       t/004-get-user.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.005
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0, c+1), with c the current value and c+1
           at most NZERO-1, returns 0 and getpriority() then reports c+1;
           when it reports c instead, c is the implementation's highest
           value (asking for NZERO-1 leaves it at c).
note       Q-86: the highest value the implementation enforces may be below NZERO-1.
test       t/005-set-raise.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.006
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A value above the highest supported one is not an error: 
           setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0, 4*NZERO) returns 0 and the value
           read back is from the old value up to NZERO-1 and does not
           change if the request is repeated.
note       Q-86: the highest value the implementation enforces may be below NZERO-1.
test       t/006-set-clamps.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.007
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, pid, v) for another process sets that
           process: a child asked to report its own value afterwards gets
           the value asked for (or the implementation's highest value), and
           the parent's getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, pid) agrees.
test       t/007-set-other-process.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.008
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       setpriority(PRIO_PGRP, pgid, v) sets every process of the group:
           after raising both members of the two-child group above, both
           report the same value, not below the larger of their old values
           and not above v.
test       t/008-set-pgrp.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.009
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#ERRORS/ESRCH
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       When no process matches, getpriority() and setpriority() return
           -1 with ESRCH: PRIO_PROCESS with the ID of a child that has been
           reaped, and PRIO_PGRP with the ID of a group whose only member
           has been reaped (tried with up to three IDs; a call that
           succeeds because the ID has been reused is skipped).
note       The test is FAIL if every ID tried matches a process.
test       t/009-esrch.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.010
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A which value that is none of the three (tried: -1, INT_MAX,
           INT_MIN; at least one must be refused) gets -1 with EINVAL from
           getpriority() and from setpriority() (run in a child).
note       An implementation may define more values, so a candidate that
           succeeds is skipped.
test       t/010-einval-which.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.011
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#ERRORS/ESRCH
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A PRIO_PROCESS who that cannot be a process ID ((id_t)-1) makes
           getpriority() and setpriority() return -1 with EINVAL or ESRCH.
note       Both errors apply (no process, not a valid ID); XSH 2.3 lets the
           implementation choose.
test       t/011-who-invalid.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.012
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#ERRORS/EACCES
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       Without appropriate privileges the nice value cannot be lowered:
           after raising its value, a child calling setpriority() with a
           lower value either gets -1 (EACCES is the error the page lists;
           the errno is not checked), or returns 0, and the value read back
           is not below the one before. The test is
           UNRESOLVED when the value did get lower (the process may have
           appropriate privileges).
note       If the effective user ID is 0 the child first tries to give up
           privilege with setgid() and setuid() to an unlikely user ID.
test       t/012-set-lower.c

assertion  XSH.getpriority.013
anchor     functions/getpriority.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       The value set applies to the process as a whole: after one thread
           calls setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0, v) with v above the current
           value, getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0) called from another thread of
           the process reports what the calling thread sees (UNRESOLVED
           when the value did not go up, e.g. at the implementation's
           highest value).
test       t/013-set-process-wide.c
